{"id":"941f096c-8ea8-480f-bf56-594ca71f632d","object":"chat.completion","created":1784807006,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    if \\\"payload\\\" in case:\\n        inner = case[\\\"payload\\\"]\\n    else:\\n        inner = case\\n    tokens = inner.get(\\\"tokens\\\")\\n    calls = inner.get(\\\"calls\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(tokens, list) or not all(isinstance(t, str) for t in tokens):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"tokens must be a list of strings\\\")\\n    n = len(tokens)\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be a list\\\")\\n    valid_calls = []\\n    for c in calls:\\n        if not isinstance(c, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"each call must be a dict\\\")\\n        call_id = c.get(\\\"call_id\\\")\\n        start = c.get(\\\"start\\\")\\n        end = c.get(\\\"end\\\")\\n        score = c.get(\\\"score\\\")\\n        repl = c.get(\\\"replacement_tokens\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(call_id, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"call_id must be a string\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(start, int) or not isinstance(end, int):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"start and end must be integers\\\")\\n        if not (0 <= start < end <= n):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"invalid start/end range\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(score, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"score must be numeric\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(repl, list) or not all(isinstance(r, str) for r in repl):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"replacement_tokens must be a list of strings\\\")\\n        valid_calls.append((call_id, start, end, score, repl))\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(valid_calls, key=lambda c: (-c[3], c[1], c[0]))\\n    accepted = []\\n    accepted_intervals = []\\n    for call_id, start, end, score, repl in sorted_calls:\\n        overlap = False\\n        for (as_, ae) in accepted_intervals:\\n            if max(start, as_) < min(end, ae):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            accepted.append((call_id, start, end, score, repl))\\n            accepted_intervals.append((start, end))\\n    accepted_sorted = sorted(accepted, key=lambda x: (x[1], x[0]))\\n    selected_ids = [c[0] for c in accepted_sorted]\\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)\\n    accepted_desc = sorted(accepted, key=lambda x: -x[1])\\n    for call_id, start, end, score, repl in accepted_desc:\\n        result_tokens[start:end] = repl\\n    return {\\\"selected_ids\\\": selected_ids, \\\"result_tokens\\\": result_tokens}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing Python source code defining `solve(case)`. The function must return a JSON-serializable object. The problem: given tokens list and calls list with start, end, score, replacement_tokens, we need to select a subset of calls that do not overlap (based on start/end intervals), rank them by descending score, then start, then call_id (ascending? \"call ID\" likely string, but specify order). Greedy selection: pick highest ranked, then next that doesn't overlap any selected. Then order selected calls by start for reporting. Then apply replacements from right to left to avoid offset shifts. Return selected IDs and final interleaved token stream.\n\nThe function must not mutate case, use only standard library, no randomness etc. Must validate fields: tokens is list of strings, calls list of dicts with call_id string, start int, end int, score float, replacement_tokens list of strings. Overlap defined as intervals [start, end) exclusive? Typically end is exclusive? Given example: start=1 end=3 covers tokens indices 1,2; start=2 end=5 covers 2,3,4; they overlap at index 2. So overlap if max(start1, start2) < min(end1, end2). Greedy: sort calls by rank key (score descending, start ascending, call_id ascending) then iterate, accept if no overlap with already accepted.\n\nAfter selection, order accepted by start (ascending) for reporting (maybe also tie by call_id?). Then apply replacements from right to left: iterate selected in reverse order of start? Actually apply from right to left so that earlier offsets don't shift. For each selected call, replace tokens[start:end] with replacement_tokens. Since we go from rightmost to leftmost, indices remain valid.\n\nReturn dict with keys like \"selected_ids\" (list of call_ids in order of source start?) and \"result_tokens\" (list of strings). Also maybe \"case_id\"? Not needed. The spec: \"Return selected IDs and the exact interleaved token stream.\" So output should have \"selected_ids\" and \"result_tokens\". Also maybe \"score\"? Not required.\n\nValidate: every call must have required fields, tokens must be list of strings, start and end within range (0 <= start < end <= len(tokens)), replacement_tokens must be list of strings, score must be number. Also we need to ensure that if no calls, selected_ids empty and result_tokens same as original. Raise ValueError for infeasible (like missing fields, invalid types, out of bounds). Use isinstance checks.\n\nWrite solve function that takes case dict, extracts tokens and calls, validates, then performs algorithm. Return dict.\n\nMake sure to escape properly for JSON embedding. Use triple quotes? Actually as JSON string, we need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. Write code as a single line? No, we can have newlines as \\n. But easier: write code with newlines, then escape them for JSON. I'll write proper Python code and then manually escape or use json.dumps in mind. The response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value string. So I'll construct the code and then escape.\n\nImportant: The function must not use any external libraries, only standard library. Use only json for serialization? Not needed; we return dict which is JSON-serializable.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Validate