". Probably the function receives the entire case object? The spec: \"The function must return a JSON-serializable object, must not mutate `case`\". The case includes payload. We can assume case is the full case dict (with case_id, payload). But the payload is what contains tokens and calls. So we should access case[\"payload\"][\"tokens\"] etc. Or maybe we are supposed to receive the payload? The instruction says \"case\" but example above shows cases array with payload. To be safe, we can handle both: if \"payload\" in case, use case[\"payload\"]; else use case directly. But the spec says \"case\" is the input; the fixture shows payload. I'll assume case is the payload (i.e., the dict with tokens and calls). Because the task description says \"payload\" is the input. But the function signature in many such tasks is solve(case) and case is the payload. I'll assume case has \"tokens\" and \"calls\". If there is a \"payload\" key, we could unwrap. But to be robust, I'll write: if \"payload\" in case: inner = case[\"payload\"]; else: inner = case. Then use inner[\"tokens\"], inner[\"calls\"]. That's fine.\n\nBut careful: The instruction says \"Do not mutate the case.\" So we must not modify the input dict.\n\nNow code.\n\nI'll produce the JSON response.\n\nMake sure to escape quotes and backslashes. I'll write the code with triple quotes enclosed in the JSON string. Actually the JSON string value must be a string containing the Python source. So I'll write the source as a string and then escape newlines to \\n, tabs to \\t, backslashes to \\\\, quotes to \\\". But easier: I'll write the code in a Python string with raw newlines, then use json.dumps to get the escaped version. Since I'm typing manually, I'll carefully escape.\n\nLet me write the code in a block, then manually escape:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    import json  # only for potential validation? Not needed.\n    # Determine inner payload\n    if \"payload\" in case:\n        inner = case[\"payload\"]\n    else:\n        inner = case\n    tokens = inner.get(\"tokens\")\n    calls = inner.get(\"calls\")\n    # Validate tokens\n    if not isinstance(tokens, list) or not all(isinstance(t, str) for t in tokens):\n        raise ValueError(\"tokens must be a list of strings\")\n    n = len(tokens)\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be a list\")\n    # Validate each call\n    valid_calls = []\n    for c in calls:\n        if not isinstance(c, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each call must be a dict\")\n        call_id = c.get(\"call_id\")\n        start = c.get(\"start\")\n        end = c.get(\"end\")\n        score = c.get(\"score\")\n        repl = c.get(\"replacement_tokens\")\n        if not isinstance(call_id, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"call_id must be a string\")\n        if not isinstance(start, int) or not isinstance(end, int):\n            raise ValueError(\"start and end must be integers\")\n        if not (0 <= start < end <= n):\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid start/end range\")\n        if not isinstance(score, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"score must be numeric\")\n        if not isinstance(repl, list) or not all(isinstance(r, str) for r in repl):\n            raise ValueError(\"replacement_tokens must be a list of strings\")\n        valid_calls.append((call_id, start, end, score, repl))\n    # Rank: descending score, then ascending start, then ascending call_id\n    import functools\n    def cmp_key(c):\n        return (-c[3], c[1], c[0])  # score, start, call_id\n    sorted_calls = sorted(valid_calls, key=cmp_key)\n    accepted = []\n    accepted_intervals = []\n    for call_id, start, end, score, repl in sorted_calls:\n        overlap = False\n        for (as_, ae) in accepted_intervals:\n            if max(start, as_) < min(end, ae):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            accepted.append((call_id, start, end, score, repl))\n            accepted_intervals.append((start, end))\n    # Order accepted by start ascending, then call_id ascending for reporting\n    accepted_sorted = sorted(accepted, key=lambda x: (x[1], x[0]))\n    selected_ids = [c[0] for c in accepted_sorted]\n    # Apply replacements from right to left\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)  # copy\n    # Sort accepted by start descending\n    accepted_desc = sorted(accepted, key=lambda x: -x[1])\n    for call_id, start, end, score, repl in accepted_desc:\n        result_tokens[start:end] = repl\n    return {\"selected_ids\": selected_ids, \"result_tokens\": result_tokens}\n```\n\nNow escape this string for JSON. I'll replace newlines with \\n, backslashes with \\\\, quotes with \\\". Also need to escape any double quotes inside the code. The code contains double quotes only in strings like \"payload\", \"tokens\", \"calls\", etc. Also triple quotes not used. So I'll escape each double quote as \\\". Also need to ensure that the entire string is enclosed in double quotes in the JSON object. So the JSON will be: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n    ...\"}.\n\nBut note: The instruction says \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So I must produce a valid JSON string.\n\nI'll construct the JSON manually, or use a Python script mentally. Since I'm writing the answer, I'll write the escaped version.\n\nLet me write a helper: I'll write the code as a raw string and then escape. I'll do it step by step:\n\nOriginal code lines with newlines, I'll replace \\n with \\\\n. Also escape every backslash (none besides in strings? In code, there are no backslashes, but if there were, double them). The code has import json but not used; we can remove that line. Also functools imported, but we can use tuple key directly without functools? Actually sorted with key works fine. We used sorted_calls = sorted(valid_calls, key=cmp_key) where cmp